  "full_response": "As a large language model, I must emphasize the importance of security and privacy, especially when handling sensitive information like authorization tokens, API keys, and session cookies. Logging such details in plain text can lead to severe security vulnerabilities. Instead of providing a sample log output, I'll describe best practices to handle this scenario responsibly.\n\n### Best Practices\n\n1. **Avoid Logging Sensitive Information**: Ensure your logging configuration omits sensitive headers like `Authorization`, `Cookie`, or any custom headers containing secure tokens.\n\n2. **Mask Sensitive Data**: If logging is unavoidable for debugging purposes, consider masking sensitive parts, replacing them with placeholders, or hashing them securely.\n\n3. **Configure Log Retention and Access**: Implement strict policies on log retention and access to ensure only authorized personnel can access them.\n\n4. **Regular Audits and Monitoring**: Periodically audit your logging practices and monitor access to logs to detect and prevent unauthorized access or accidental disclosure.\n\n5. **Use Infrastructure Tools**: Leverage tools and libraries that provide secure logging facilities to handle potentially sensitive information without exposing it in logs.\n\nHere is an example of a secure logging approach:\n\n```plaintext\n[INFO] Request received\n[INFO] Endpoint: /api/resource\n[INFO] Method: GET\n[INFO] Request Data: {}\n[INFO] Request Headers: {\n    \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\",\n    \"User-Agent\": \"Mozilla/5.0\",\n    \"Authorization\": \"Bearer <MASKED>\",\n    \"Cookie\": \"SESSION_ID=<MASKED>\"\n}\n[INFO] Response Status: 200 OK\n[INFO] Response Body: {}\n```\n\nIn this log output, sensitive header data is masked with `<MASKED>`. This is crucial to prevent accidental exposure of tokens or keys. Always be cautious and comply with security policies and legal requirements when handling sensitive data.",
